  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":20,"t":"single_choice","p":"Which of these describes the shaded region ''elements that are in A but not in B''?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which of these describes the shaded region ''elements that are in A but not in B''?"}]}]},"e":"In set notation this is A ∩ B′, read ''A intersect not-B''. Writing A′ ∩ B gives the opposite region, and A − B is only equivalent when the sets are disjoint.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"sets and Venn diagrams","st":"set notation for a region","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":20,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Translate the words into symbols one at a time: ''in A'' → A, ''not in B'' → B′, and ''and'' → ∩.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","option_labels":["A ∩ B′","A′ ∩ B","A ∪ B","A ∩ B"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":21,"t":"single_choice","p":"A tap fills a tank at 12 litres per minute. How many minutes does it take to fill a 9 000-litre tank?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A tap fills a tank at 12 litres per minute. How many minutes does it take to fill a 9 000-litre tank?"}]}]},"e":"9 000 ÷ 12 = 750 minutes. Dividing the other way (12 ÷ 9 000) gives a meaningless 0.0013, which is the unit-rate error of dividing the rate by the total.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"rate","st":"rate and time","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":21,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"When the rate is ''per minute'', the time is total ÷ rate. Check the size of your answer against the setting.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","option_labels":["750 minutes","0.0013 minutes (divides the rate by the total)","108 000 minutes (multiplies instead of dividing)","75 minutes (loses a factor of 10)"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":22,"t":"single_choice","p":"A bag contains 7 counters: 3 red, 2 green and 2 yellow. One counter is taken at random. Which event has probability 4/7?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A bag contains 7 counters: 3 red, 2 green and 2 yellow. One counter is taken at random. Which event has probability 4/7?"}]}]},"e":"''Not red'' removes only the 3 red counters, leaving 4, so P(not red) = 4/7. P(red) = 3/7 and P(green) = P(yellow) = 2/7, so the other options are the complementary and per-colour values.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"probability","st":"complementary events","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":22,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"P(not A) = 1 − P(A). Count the outcomes that are NOT in the event before dividing.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","option_labels":["Taking a counter that is not red","Taking a red counter","Taking a green counter","Taking a green or yellow counter"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":23,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A student writes: ''The ratio 6 : 9 simplifies to 2 : 3, so to make 12 litres of paint I add 2 litres of blue to 3 litres of yellow.''\n\n(a) Identify the error in the student''s reasoning.\n(b) Calculate the correct amounts of blue and yellow for 12 litres.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A student writes: ''The ratio 6 : 9 simplifies to 2 : 3, so to make 12 litres of paint I add 2 litres of blue to 3 litres of yellow.''"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Identify the error in the student''s reasoning.\n(b) Calculate the correct amounts of blue and yellow for 12 litres."}]}]},"e":"2 : 3 has 5 parts in total. 12 ÷ 5 = 2.4 litres per part, so blue = 2 × 2.4 = 4.8 litres and yellow = 3 × 2.4 = 7.2 litres. They add to 12 litres, and 4.8 : 7.2 simplifies to 2 : 3.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"ratio","st":"ratio","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":23,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"A ratio gives parts, not amounts. Scale the parts so that their SUM is the total required, then check by simplifying.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Identify the error in one sentence.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Compare 2 + 3 with the 12 litres required.","A simplified ratio gives proportional parts, not quantities."],"strand":"C(i)","accepted":["the student treated the ratio numbers as litres instead of scaling them to a total of 12 litres"],"keywords":[["student"],["treated"],["ratio"],["numbers"],["litres"],["instead"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the litres of blue needed for 12 litres.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["How many equal parts are in the ratio 2 : 3?","Divide 12 litres by the number of parts, then multiply by 2."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":4.8,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"litres"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the litres of yellow needed for 12 litres.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Yellow takes 3 parts.","Multiply one part by 3, and check that blue + yellow = 12 litres."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":7.2,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"litres"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for identifying that ratio numbers were used as amounts. (b) 2 marks: 1 for finding one part (2.4), 1 for 4.8 litres. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 7.2 litres, 1 for the check that the total is 12 litres.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for identifying that ratio numbers were used as amounts. (b) 2 marks: 1 for finding one part (2.4), 1 for 4.8 litres. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 7.2 litres, 1 for the check that the total is 12 litres.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"In (a) say what the student did wrong, not only that the answer is wrong. Check in (c) that the two amounts add to 12 litres."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for identifying that ratio numbers were used as amounts. (b) 2 marks: 1 for finding one part (2.4), 1 for 4.8 litres. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 7.2 litres, 1 for the check that the total is 12 litres."},{"n":24,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A spinner has 8 equal sectors numbered 1 to 8. A student claims: ''P(even) = 4/8 and P(prime) = 4/8, so P(even or prime) = 1.''\n\n(a) List the outcomes that are both even and prime.\n(b) Explain why the student''s addition double-counts.\n(c) Calculate the correct probability of even or prime.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A spinner has 8 equal sectors numbered 1 to 8. A student claims: ''P(even) = 4/8 and P(prime) = 4/8, so P(even or prime) = 1.''"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) List the outcomes that are both even and prime.\n(b) Explain why the student''s addition double-counts.\n(c) Calculate the correct probability of even or prime."}]}]},"e":"Even = {2, 4, 6, 8}, prime = {2, 3, 5, 7}. The only overlap is 2, so adding 4/8 + 4/8 counts it twice. P(even or prime) = 4/8 + 4/8 − 1/8 = 7/8, matching the seven distinct outcomes.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"probability","st":"probability","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":24,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). Whenever two events can happen together, subtract the overlap.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State how many outcomes are both even and prime.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Test each number 1 to 8 against both conditions.","2 is even and prime; 4, 6 and 8 are even but not prime."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":1,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Explain why adding the two probabilities double-counts.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Which outcome appears in both lists?","The addition rule must subtract the overlap, as in a two-set Venn diagram."],"strand":"C(ii)","accepted":["2 is counted in both events, so it is added twice"],"keywords":[["counted"],["events"],["added"],["twice"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the correct probability of even or prime, as a fraction in simplest form.","kind":"expression","points":2,"hints":["Add the two probabilities and subtract the probability of the overlap.","Count the outcomes in the union: 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8."],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["7/8"]}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 2. (b) 2 marks: 1 for naming the overlapping outcome, 1 for explaining the double count. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the method, 1 for 7/8.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 2. (b) 2 marks: 1 for naming the overlapping outcome, 1 for explaining the double count. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the method, 1 for 7/8.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"List the outcomes explicitly in (a); that list is the evidence for your explanation in (b)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 2. (b) 2 marks: 1 for naming the overlapping outcome, 1 for explaining the double count. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the method, 1 for 7/8."},{"n":25,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A shop increases every price by 20%. One month later it reduces every price by 20% of the new price. A shirt originally costing $250 is discussed.\n\n(a) Calculate the price after the increase.\n(b) Calculate the price after the reduction.\n(c) Explain why the final price is not $250.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A shop increases every price by 20%. One month later it reduces every price by 20% of the new price. A shirt originally costing "},{"type":"text","text":"$250 is discussed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the price after the increase.\n(b) Calculate the price after the reduction.\n(c) Explain why the final price is not "},{"type":"text","text":"$250."}]}]},"e":"$250 × 1.2 = $300; then $300 × 0.8 = $240. The reduction is $60 while the increase was $50, because each percentage is calculated on a different base. The combined multiplier 0.96 shows an overall 4% decrease.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"proportion","st":"proportion","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":25,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Percentages apply to the amount that exists at that moment. Chain the multipliers (1.2 × 0.8 = 0.96) rather than adding percentages.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the price after the 20% increase, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["A 20% increase multiplies the price by 1.20.","20% of 250 is 50."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":300,"tolerance":0,"unit":"$"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the price after the 20% reduction, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The reduction is 20% of the NEW price, not of $250.","Multiply 300 by 0.80."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":240,"tolerance":0,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why the final price is not $250.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["What amount is the 20% reduction calculated from?","Compare 20% of 250 with 20% of 300."],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["the 20% reduction is taken from the higher price of $300, so it removes $60 instead of the $50 that was added"],"keywords":[["reduction"],["taken"],["higher"],["price"],["removes"],["instead"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for $300. (b) 2 marks: 1 for using $300 as the base, 1 for $240. (c) 2 marks: 1 for identifying the different bases, 1 for a clear consequence ($60 removed vs $50 added).","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for $300. (b) 2 marks: 1 for using $300 as the base, 1 for $240. (c) 2 marks: 1 for identifying the different bases, 1 for a clear consequence ($60 removed vs $50 added).","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Show the multiplier you use in (a) and (b). In (c) refer to the base each percentage is taken from."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for $300. (b) 2 marks: 1 for using $300 as the base, 1 for $240. (c) 2 marks: 1 for identifying the different bases, 1 for a clear consequence ($60 removed vs $50 added)."},{"n":26,"t":"assess_parts","p":"In a two-set Venn diagram, n(A only) = 7, n(B only) = 9 and n(A ∪ B) = 21.\n\n(a) Explain how the three regions of the union are related.\n(b) Calculate n(A ∩ B).\n(c) A student says ''A ∩ B must be the bigger region because it is in both circles''. Correct the statement using your value.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a two-set Venn diagram, n(A only) = 7, n(B only) = 9 and n(A ∪ B) = 21."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Explain how the three regions of the union are related.\n(b) Calculate n(A ∩ B).\n(c) A student says ''A ∩ B must be the bigger region because it is in both circles''. Correct the statement using your value."}]}]},"e":"The three regions inside the circles are disjoint and together make up A ∪ B. So n(A∩B) = 21 − 7 − 9 = 5. The intersection is a region in its own right and its size comes from the data, not from belonging to two sets.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"sets and Venn diagrams","st":"sets and Venn diagrams","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":26,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"In a Venn diagram, every region is counted once. Write the addition for the whole union and solve for the unknown region.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Explain how the three regions of A ∪ B are related.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Which regions lie inside the two circles?","They do not overlap each other, so their numbers add to the union."],"strand":"C(i)","accepted":["the union is the sum of A only, B only and the intersection"]},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate n(A ∩ B).","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Write 21 = 7 + 9 + x.","Subtract the two ''only'' values from 21."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":5,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Correct the student''s statement using your value.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare 5 with 7 and 9.","Explain what ''in both circles'' actually describes."],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["the intersection holds only 5 students, fewer than either ''only'' region, because being in both sets is a separate region"],"keywords":[["intersection"],["holds"],["students"],["fewer"],["either"],["''only''"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for the partition idea. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the equation, 1 for 5. (c) 2 marks: 1 for comparing 5 with 7 and 9, 1 for explaining why the overlap is a separate region.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for the partition idea. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the equation, 1 for 5. (c) 2 marks: 1 for comparing 5 with 7 and 9, 1 for explaining why the overlap is a separate region.","response_tools":["venn","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"venn","response_guidance":"Sketch the diagram in the workspace to support your explanation, then answer the parts in the boxes."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for the partition idea. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the equation, 1 for 5. (c) 2 marks: 1 for comparing 5 with 7 and 9, 1 for explaining why the overlap is a separate region."},{"n":27,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Two cars set off together on the same 240 km route. Car A travels at 80 km/h and Car B at 60 km/h, both at a steady speed.\n\n(a) Calculate the journey time of Car A, in hours.\n(b) Calculate the journey time of Car B, in hours.\n(c) Calculate how many minutes later Car B arrives.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two cars set off together on the same 240 km route. Car A travels at 80 km/h and Car B at 60 km/h, both at a steady speed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the journey time of Car A, in hours.\n(b) Calculate the journey time of Car B, in hours.\n(c) Calculate how many minutes later Car B arrives."}]}]},"e":"Car A takes 240 ÷ 80 = 3 hours and Car B takes 240 ÷ 60 = 4 hours, so B arrives 1 hour, that is 60 minutes, later. Comparing speeds would not give a time.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"rate","st":"rate","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":27,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Time is distance ÷ speed. When comparing two journeys, compare the times you compute, not the speeds.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the journey time of Car A, in hours.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["time = distance ÷ speed.","240 ÷ 80."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":3,"tolerance":0,"unit":"hours"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the journey time of Car B, in hours.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Use Car B''s speed of 60 km/h.","240 ÷ 60."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":4,"tolerance":0,"unit":"hours"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate how many minutes later Car B arrives.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Subtract the two times, not the two speeds.","Convert the difference in hours to minutes."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":60,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 3 hours. (b) 1 mark for 4 hours. (c) 2 marks: 1 for subtracting the times, 1 for 60 minutes.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 3 hours. (b) 1 mark for 4 hours. (c) 2 marks: 1 for subtracting the times, 1 for 60 minutes.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Write the formula you use in (a) and (b), with the numbers substituted."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 3 hours. (b) 1 mark for 4 hours. (c) 2 marks: 1 for subtracting the times, 1 for 60 minutes."},{"n":28,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A fair coin is tossed three times and the sequence of faces is recorded.\n\n(a) Calculate the number of outcomes in the sample space.\n(b) List the outcomes that contain exactly two heads.\n(c) Calculate the probability of exactly two heads.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A fair coin is tossed three times and the sequence of faces is recorded."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the number of outcomes in the sample space.\n(b) List the outcomes that contain exactly two heads.\n(c) Calculate the probability of exactly two heads."}]}]},"e":"2³ = 8 equally likely sequences. Exactly two heads: HHT, HTH, THH, so P = 3/8. Grouping by ''number of heads'' would not be equally likely and must not be used as the sample space.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"probability","st":"probability","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":28,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"For repeated independent trials the sample space size multiplies; list the outcomes systematically so none are missed.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the number of outcomes in the sample space.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Each toss has 2 possible faces.","Multiply the possibilities for the three tosses."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":8,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State how many sequences have exactly two heads.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Work systematically: start with the tail in each position.","There are three arrangements of two H and one T."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":3,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the probability of exactly two heads.","kind":"expression","points":2,"hints":["Count your outcomes from (b) and divide by the sample space size.","3 favourable outcomes out of 8."],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["3/8"]}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 8. (b) 2 marks: 2 for all three sequences, 1 for two of them. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 3 favourable outcomes, 1 for 3/8.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 8. (b) 2 marks: 2 for all three sequences, 1 for two of them. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 3 favourable outcomes, 1 for 3/8.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"A table of the eight sequences is a good way to be sure you have listed them all in (b)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 8. (b) 2 marks: 2 for all three sequences, 1 for two of them. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 3 favourable outcomes, 1 for 3/8."}]'::jsonb) as x
